# The project video. Every frame is the real plugin rendering through the real
# FFGL sequence — this is the generator being *operated*, not a mock-up.
#
#   ./build/burintest --sequence /tmp/burin-a --script tools/video.cues \
#                     --file docs/example-plate.svg --size 1920x1080 \
#                     --seconds 42 --fps 30
#
# Syntax: `T Name=V` sets at a time, `T1..T2 Name=V1..V2` ramps between two
# (eased, because a parameter that starts and stops abruptly reads as a cut).
#
# Continuous values are the 0..1 the host sees, not the physical quantity — see
# Controls.h for why there is no other kind. Counts and dropdowns hold their
# real value.
#
# ## The order is the order of the argument
#
# The hard part of pitching this plugin is that its central claim is invisible
# in a still. "Sharp at any size" is not a look; it is the *absence* of an
# artefact, and an audience cannot see an absence unless they have just been
# shown the thing it replaces. So the film opens by going somewhere a bitmap
# cannot follow and staying there long enough to be believed, and only then
# moves on to the things that are visible in a frame.
#
# Then, in order: that the paths are still separate (stroke and fill), that they
# have length (the write-on), that they can be addressed individually (stagger,
# order, colour walk), and finally that all of it moves on a clock.

# ------------------------------------------------ 0s: the whole drawing, at rest
# Paper, black ink, fitted to its own content. No motion at all for two seconds:
# the eye needs a reference frame before it can be told anything has changed,
# and this is also the frame the thumbnail is cut from.
0.0    Background Alpha=1.0
0.0    Background=0.97
0.0    Background_Green=0.96
0.0    Background_Blue=0.93
0.0    Bounds=1
0.0    Fit=0
0.0    Zoom=0.5
0.0    Detail=0.5
0.0    Min Stroke px=0.28
0.0    Sync=0
0.0    Rate=0.30
0.0    Wave=0

# ------------------------------------------- 2.5s: in, past where a bitmap dies
# Six seconds of continuous zoom to about 12x. Slow on purpose. The point is not
# the destination but that nothing softens on the way — a cut straight to the
# close-up would be indistinguishable from a second, larger export.
#
# The ladder crosses about seven rungs across this ramp, so seven rebuilds
# happen while it plays. If any of them is visible as a hitch, this is the shot
# that will show it, which is the other reason it is here and this long.
2.5..8.5    Zoom=0.5..0.79
3.0..8.5    Position X=0.5..0.545
3.0..8.5    Position Y=0.5..0.470

# Hold at the bottom of the dive. The centre scale is drawn at 0.4 units of
# stroke — sub-pixel at 1:1 — and here it is a clean line. That is the whole
# argument, and it needs a beat to land.
8.5..11.0   Zoom=0.79..0.79

# --------------------------------------------------- 11s: back out, still clean
11.0..14.0  Zoom=0.79..0.5
11.0..14.0  Position X=0.545..0.5
11.0..14.0  Position Y=0.470..0.5

# ------------------------------------------ 14s: the paths are still separate
# Fill only, then stroke only. On this drawing the difference is stark: the
# cardinal arrows and the star points are fill-with-no-stroke and vanish under
# Stroke Only, while everything else is stroke-with-no-fill and vanishes under
# Fill Only. Two genuinely different pictures out of one file, and neither is an
# edge detect.
14.0   Draw=1
16.5   Draw=2
16.5   Min Stroke px=0.42

# ---------------------------------------------- 19s: they also have a LENGTH
# The write-on. Held rather than clock-driven, so the cue sheet controls it
# exactly and it lands where the cut wants it. Ordered longest-first, so the
# rings and the long arcs arrive before the degree ticks — the drawing builds
# structure first and detail last, which is the order somebody would draw it in.
19.0   Reveal=3
19.0   Order=2
19.0   Stagger=0.52
19.0   Fill Fade=0.4
19.0   Progress=0.0
19.0..27.0  Progress=0.0..1.0

# ------------------------------------------- 27s: addressed one shape at a time
# The colour walk, applied to strokes only, spread across the drawing so the
# hue moves shape by shape rather than washing the whole thing one colour.
27.0   Recolour=2
27.0   Colour=0.20
27.0   Colour_Green=0.55
27.0   Colour_Blue=1.0
27.0..30.0  Colour Spread=0.5..0.80

# Isolate: the same drawing reduced to a slice of itself and brought back. This
# is the control that is hardest to explain in text and instant on screen.
30.0   First Shape=7
30.0..33.0  Shape Count=8..464

# ------------------------------------------------ 33s: and all of it moves
# Back to the file's own colours on the dark ground, with the motion running:
# a slow breath on the zoom and a drift, both on the same clock, the two drift
# axes a quarter cycle apart so it wanders rather than sliding on a diagonal.
33.0   Recolour=3
33.0   Colour=0.55
33.0   Colour_Green=0.82
33.0   Colour_Blue=1.0
33.0   Colour Spread=0.62
33.0   Background=0.03
33.0   Background_Green=0.07
33.0   Background_Blue=0.16
33.0   Rate=0.34
33.0..35.0  Zoom Move=0.0..0.30
33.0..35.0  Drift X=0.0..0.22
33.0..35.0  Drift Y=0.0..0.16
33.0..36.0  Spin=0.5..0.545

# The reveal runs again underneath the motion, this time scattered, so the last
# ten seconds are the plugin doing several things at once — which is what it
# actually looks like in use, and the only honest way to end.
36.0   Order=4
36.0   Stagger=0.42
36.0..41.5  Progress=0.15..1.0
